From b3ec9f8d19159805149f3f7fcdf02a7168272cb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:34:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/8] network: bind an interface uplink to its device

An interface uplink is turned into the first IPv4 address the interface
holds, so its sockets keep that address across a DHCP renewal or an
IPv6 privacy address change and get no IPv6 at all. Bind them to the
device: curl through "if!<device>", Python sockets of both families,
stream and datagram, with SO_BINDTODEVICE, while an address or prefix
uplink binds the matching address of the socket's family. A request
whose uplink is not available fails. A Python destination counts as
local by its address, or, given as a name, when it has no dot, which
needs no lookup.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
